Here you go — **the simplest, cleanest local-class example** you can possibly write.  
This is **far simpler** than your screenshot version but still shows the exact concept.

---

# ✅ **Super Simple Local Class Code**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void demo() {

    // Local class inside the function
    class Test {
        int x;
    public:
        void set(int n) { x = n; }
        int get() { return x; }
    };

    Test t;      // Allowed here
    t.set(50);
    cout << "Value inside demo(): " << t.get() << endl;
}

int main() {

    demo();      // Works

    // Test t;  // ❌ Error: Test is not visible here

    return 0;
}
```

---

# 💡 Why this is simpler?

- Only **one function** using a local class  
- Only **one variable**  
- No extra printing  
- No multiple classes  

---

# 📌 Output

```
Value inside demo(): 50
```

